    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11330": {
      "title": "Full Researcher",
      "description": "Full Researcher - Center for Community Schooling (JPF11330) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Full Researcher - Center for Community Schooling (JPF11330) Full Researcher - Center for Community Schooling Apply now to Full Researcher - Center for Community Schooling Job #JPF11330 Education / Education & Information Studies / UCLA Position overview Position title: Full Researcher Salary range: See Table 13-A and Table 13-B for the salary range for this position. The anticipated salary range is $188,500-$203,500. Application Window Open date: September 17, 2026 Next review date: Saturday, Oct 17, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Saturday, Oct 17, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date. Position description Overview of the UCLA Center for Community Schooling The UCLA Center for Community Schooling advances democracy, justice, and public education by helping schools become vibrant, community-centered places where young people learn and thrive. The Center brings research, policy, and practice into close partnership with students, families, educators, and public agencies to support meaningful and lasting school transformation. Grounded in long-term research–practice partnerships with the UCLA Community School and the Mann UCLA Community School, the Center has developed a nationally recognized approach for university-assisted community schools. At the state level, the Center plays a leadership role in California’s Community Schools Partnership Program (CCSPP), supporting over 2,000 schools and more than one million students and families. Through applied research, technical assistance, and policy engagement, the Center demonstrates how universities can serve as powerful partners in advancing equitable, community-driven change. Description of Position In collaboration with the Faculty Co-Director, the Researcher/Co-Director provides scholarly and research leadership for the UCLA Center for Community Schooling. The incumbent develops, leads, and conducts a program of research focused on community schooling, research–practice partnerships, educational equity, and school transformation. The Co-Director is expected to produce significant scholarly contributions, including peer-reviewed publications and other research products; develop and lead externally funded research; disseminate findings to scholarly, policy, and practitioner audiences; and contribute professional and public service based on their research expertise. The Co-Director leads and participates in interdisciplinary and community-engaged research–practice partnerships with schools, districts, public agencies, and other partners. The incumbent exercises substantial intellectual independence in developing research questions, theoretical and conceptual frameworks, methodological approaches, analyses, and scholarly products and is expected to establish and maintain a significant record of research accomplishment and professional recognition. In their role as Co-Director, the incumbent also provides leadership necessary to support the Center's research mission, including coordination of research personnel, research partnerships, sponsored research, and the integration of the Center's research portfolio. Core Responsibilities Research and Scholarly Contributions Provide oversight and leadership for the Center’s research portfolio including qualitative, quantitative, and mixed-methods studies Advance research–practice partnerships that: Inform school-level practice Ensure accountability Generate generalizable knowledge about community schooling Lead the development of research products, including reports, policy briefs, and scholarly publications Ensure the translation of research findings into actionable insights for educators, policymakers, and communities through peer-reviewed publications, policy briefs, technical reports, and other publicly available publication channels. Oversee research administration, including review processes, compliance, and coordination with district partners Supervise research personnel and ensure appropriate staffing is maintained to meet research deliverables. Oversee research budgets and sponsored-project implementation. Ensure compliance with UCLA and external policies and procedures. Development and alignment of the Center’s research, policy, and practice agendas. Seek extramural funding and serve and PI/co-PI on contracts and grants that support the research mission of the center. With the Faculty Co-Director, Co-lead the strategic direction of the Center, advancing its research mission to support equitable, community-centered school transformation. Continuously seek opportunities to expand the Center’s influence at local, state, and national levels. Cultivate partnerships with school districts, state agencies, and national organizations Provide research mentorship to graduate and undergraduate students working in the Center to expand their knowledge and experience in community-based research and practice. Foster pathways for students to engage meaningfully in research–practice partnerships Statewide and Systems-Level Engagement Provide leadership in statewide initiatives, including California’s Community Schools Partnership Program Collaborate with state agencies, technical assistance providers, and local educational agencies to support implementation and continuous improvement Monitor and contribute to policy development related to community schooling and aligned reform efforts Participate in and help lead networks that advance community school policy and practice at the state and national levels Practice Partnerships and School Engagement Maintain and strengthen long-term partnerships with UCLA Community Schools and affiliated sites. Collaborate with educators and school leaders to support continuous improvement and innovation. Contribute to publishing research and findings that can inform community school practices including in spaces of teaching and learning, family and community engagement, integrated student supports, and collaborative leadership. Scholarly dissemination and Field building Disseminate the Center’s research and findings to scholarly, practitioner, policy, and public audiences. Represent the Center and present research findings at scholarly and professional conferences, convenings, and policy forums. Contribute to professional networks and scholarly communities related to community schooling, educational equity, and research-practice partnerships. Contribute research expertise to public and professional discussions concerning community schooling. Develop communication strategies that elevate community school practices and outcomes Oversee the production of reports, policy briefs, and other materials that share the Center’s work broadly. Qualifications Basic qualifications Doctorate (Ph.D., Ed.D., or equivalent) in education, public policy, or a related field Demonstrated expertise in research design and methodology Experience leading or contributing to research–practice partnerships Strong record of significant and original research and scholarly publication, including peer-reviewed scholarship Demonstrated leadership experience in education or related fields Demonstrated ability to exercise intellectual independence in conceptualizing, designing, conducting, and disseminating research At least 5 years of progressively responsible leadership experience in research centers, research programs, research-practice partnerships, or related scholarly organizations Preferred qualifications Deep knowledge of K–12 education systems, equity-focused reform, and community schooling Experience working with school districts, state agencies, or community-based organizations Experience translating research into policy and practice Demonstrated success in grant writing and project leadership Commitment to community-engaged scholarship and collaborative work Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Self-Statement UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service Sample Publications (Optional) Research Statement (Optional) Reference check authorization release form -   Complete and upload the reference check authorization release form Reference requirements 5 required (contact information only) Applicants must provide the names and contact information of their references. The hiring unit will request confidential letters from the references of those applicants who are under serious consideration. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11330 Help contact: mercado@seis.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Full Researcher - Center for Community Schooling or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11332": {
      "title": "Project Scientist Position in Hematology-Oncology",
      "description": "Project Scientist Position in Hematology-Oncology - Garon Lab 2026-2027 (JPF11332) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Project Scientist Position in Hematology-Oncology - Garon Lab 2026-2027 (JPF11332) Project Scientist Position in Hematology-Oncology - Garon Lab 2026-2027 Apply now to Project Scientist Position in Hematology-Oncology - Garon Lab 2026-2027 Job #JPF11332 Medicine-Hematology-Oncology / David Geffen School Of Medicine / UCLA Position overview Salary range: See Table 37-B . A reasonable estimate for this position is $79,400-$209,000. Application Window Open date: September 18, 2026 Next review date: Monday, Oct 19, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Dec 30, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Edward Garon Lab in the Division of Hematology-Oncology, Department of Medicine at the David Geffen School of Medicine at UCLA is seeking applicants for a Project Scientist position at the Assistant, Associate, or full rank. The candidate will perform collaborative research on either biomedical informatics and computational biology in lung cancer or will lead a translational research project in the area of cancer immunotherapy. This position requires a Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computational biology, systems biology, immunology and/or related fields. Five years of experience in biomedical research is preferred. This is a 12-month position which may be renewed. The Project Scientist will be responsible for developing research concepts, identifying biological mechanisms, and performing biomedical informatics and computational biology studies related to the pathogenesis of lung cancer. Alternatively, the candidate may develop and lead analyses evaluating the potential preclinical efficacy of novel immunotherapeutic approaches. Additional responsibilities include preparing high-impact scientific publications and assisting with grant preparation. The successful candidate must have documented experience working with large datasets, including DNA and RNA sequencing data. The candidate should also demonstrate the ability to work effectively within a collaborative research environment and have experience training, advising, and leading research personnel. The Project Scientist will report directly to the Principal Investigator and will conduct analyses involving large and complex datasets. The individual will train and mentor undergraduate students, graduate students, and new postdoctoral trainees in the laboratory. The position requires the ability to manage multiple projects efficiently and effectively, assist with the preparation of grant proposals, and deliver presentations to diverse scientific audiences. The candidate will actively participate in weekly team meetings, prepare manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed scientific journals, communicate effectively with scientists at UCLA and collaborating institutions, and maintain and organize critical research databases. The shared values of the DGSOM are expressed in the Cultural North Star, which was developed by members of our community and affirms our unswerving commitment to doing what’s right, making things better, and being kind. These are the standards to which we hold ourselves, and one another. Please read more about this important DGSOM program at Cultural North Star . Qualifications Basic qualifications A Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computational biology, systems biology, immunology and/or related fields is required. Preferred qualifications Five years of experience in biomedical research is preferred. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service. Reference requirements None Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11332 Help contact: kevinwashington@mednet.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Project Scientist Position in Hematology-Oncology - Garon Lab 2026-2027 or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
