    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11325": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Teaching in Youth Behavioral Health in the UCLA Department of PsychologyApply nowto Assistant Professor of Teaching in Youth Behavioral Health in the UCLA Department of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Teaching in Youth Behavioral Health in the UCLA Department of Psychology (JPF11325) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Assistant Professor of Teaching in Youth Behavioral Health in the UCLA Department of Psychology (JPF11325) Assistant Professor of Teaching in Youth Behavioral Health in the UCLA Department of Psychology Apply now to Assistant Professor of Teaching in Youth Behavioral Health in the UCLA Department of Psychology Job #JPF11325 PSYCHOLOGY / L&S Life Sciences / UCLA Position overview Position title: Assistant Professor of Teaching Salary range: The posted UC salary scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 1. The salary range for this position is $84,100-$108,900. “Off-scale salaries”, i.e. a salary that is higher than the published system-wide salary at the designated rank and step, are offered when necessary to meet competitive conditions. Application Window Open date: September 22, 2026 Next review date: Wednesday, Nov 4, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Nov 4, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date. Position description The UCLA Department of Psychology invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor of Teaching who will contribute to the development and teaching of undergraduate courses in the newly established Youth Behavioral Health undergraduate minor. Additional undergraduate courses may be taught in the area of clinical psychology. We seek applicants who demonstrate excellence in teaching, including experience employing innovative and evidence-based methods in the classroom, engaging in reflective teaching practices, and pursuing ongoing efforts to improve their teaching. Additional responsibilities include: (1) coordinating with other instructional staff who contribute to the Youth Behavioral Health Minor (e.g., Teaching Assistants, Lecturers); (2) working with other faculty and community partners to develop and maintain appropriate internship placements; (3) overseeing matching and placements for undergraduate interns; (4) managing program curricula to align with established competency models and credentials; and (5) contributing to academic standards and admission criteria for the Youth Behavioral Health Minor. In addition, the successful candidate is expected to engage in scholarly/professional activities. These activities could include contributing to research on pedagogy or contributing to research in another area with the support of undergraduate students; developing, evaluating, and disseminating innovative teaching approaches; or maintaining clinical licensure and engaging in activities related to professional clinical practice. The Assistant Professor of Teaching position is a full-time faculty position with potential security of employment akin to tenure. Those in the Professor of Teaching series are Academic Senate faculty members whose expertise and responsibilities center on undergraduate education and scholarly analysis/improvement of teaching methods. Faculty in the Professor of Teaching series are evaluated on their (1) teaching excellence, (2) professional and/or scholarly achievement and activity, and (3) university and public service. Potential applicants who have questions about the position can contact Prof. Steve S. Lee, search committee chair, at SearchCommittee@psych.ucla.edu . The UCLA Department of Psychology is located within the Division of Life Sciences, part of the College of Letters and Science that serves undergraduate and graduate students. The search is geared toward candidates who share our belief that, as professors at a leading public university, we strive for inclusive excellence in teaching, service, research, and engagement with our academic community. We are eager to welcome an outstanding scholar and mentor who will create opportunities that will deepen and enrich understanding of psychological principles and perspectives for our students, while also contributing to the collaborative and supportive environment that characterizes our department. We are also interested in applicants who will advance UCLA’s mission. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Psychology or a closely related field awarded prior to the position start date. Applicants who hold or are willing to apply for clinical licensure in California are strongly preferred. This is an Assistant Professor of Teaching position; please do not apply if you are an Associate Professor or higher (or if you are undergoing review for tenure at your university, or anticipate a tenure review during our application process) as your application will not be considered. Applicants should demonstrate a record of expertise and teaching excellence in undergraduate courses relevant to clinical psychology. Preferred qualifications include professional experience developing or delivering BA-level youth behavioral health curricula in a university setting, a record of establishing and maintaining collaborative training relationships with youth-focused mental health organizations, experience with working successfully with field placements/practica, and coordinating training with required credentialing and licensing bodies. We also value candidates who have the potential to contribute to the mentoring and service missions of the department and university, particularly in regard to improving the quality of instruction. Applicants with experience mentoring and teaching students from groups affected by disparities in educational access are strongly encouraged to apply. We seek applications from individuals with a demonstrated commitment to ensuring equal access to the benefits of their teaching and supporting the full participation of all students through consideration and appreciation of differences. The successful candidate will help foster authentic engagement and belonging within our diverse community. UCLA has scaled multiple initiatives such as Rising to the Challenge, Hispanic-Serving Institution initiative, and Native American/Pacific Islander Bruins Rising to promote inclusive excellence. UCLA is home to a diverse student body of approximately 47,000 undergraduate and graduate students and is ranked among the top national universities for social mobility. UCLA has programs to assist in partner employment, child care, schooling, and other family concerns. For additional information, visit the UCLA Academic Affairs and Personnel Office website or the UC Office of the President’s website . To ensure full consideration, the online application must be completed and submitted by November 4, 2026. Applications should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three recommenders, a statement focused on UCLA’s mission of no more than three pages, a statement of teaching philosophy of no more than three pages, and a teaching portfolio that includes syllabi, evaluations, and instructional materials used in courses previously taught. Letters of recommendation will be requested at a later date from applicants who progress to a later stage of review. To learn about the required UCLA mission statement, please see the job posting . The posted UC salary scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 1. The salary range for this position is $84,100-$108,900. “Off-scale salaries”, i.e. a salary that is higher than the published system-wide salary at the designated rank and step, are offered when necessary to meet competitive conditions. Apply at the following website: UCLA Recruit The University of California is committed to creating and maintaining a community dedicated to the advancement, application, and transmission of knowledge and creative endeavors through academic excellence, where all individuals who participate in University programs and activities can work and learn together in a safe and secure environment, free of violence, harassment, bullying and other demeaning behavior, discrimination, exploitation, or intimidation. With this commitment as well as a commitment to addressing all forms of academic misconduct, UCLA conducts targeted employment reference checks for finalists to whom departments or other hiring units would like to extend formal offers of appointment into Academic Senate faculty positions. The targeted employment reference checks involve contacting the finalists’ current and prior places of employment to ask whether there have been substantiated findings of misconduct that would violate the University’s Faculty Code of Conduct. To implement this process, UCLA requires all applicants for Academic Senate faculty positions to complete, sign, and upload the form entitled “Authorization to Release Information” into RECRUIT as part of their application. If the applicant does not include the signed authorization to release information with the application materials, the application will be considered incomplete. As with any incomplete application, the application will not receive further consideration. Although all applicants for faculty recruitments must complete the entire application, only finalists (i.e., those to whom the department or other hiring unit would like to extend a formal offer) considered for Academic Senate faculty positions will be subject to targeted employment reference checks. Qualifications Basic qualifications Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Psychology or a closely related field awarded prior to the position start date. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter Reference check authorization release form -   Complete and upload the reference check authorization release form UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service. Statement of teaching philosophy Teaching portfolio that includes syllabi, evaluations, and instructional materials used in courses previously taught Reference requirements 3 required (contact information only) Include contact information for three recommenders. Letters of recommendation will be requested at a later date from applicants who progress to a later stage of review. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11325 Help contact: scaruso@psych.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Assistant Professor of Teaching in Youth Behavioral Health in the UCLA Department of Psychology or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11352": {
      "title": "Plastic & Reconstructive Surgeon with a Craniofacial Sub-specialist (JPF11352)",
      "description": "Plastic & Reconstructive Surgeon with a Craniofacial Sub-specialist (JPF11352) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Plastic & Reconstructive Surgeon with a Craniofacial Sub-specialist (JPF11352) Plastic & Reconstructive Surgeon with a Craniofacial Sub-specialist Apply now to Plastic & Reconstructive Surgeon with a Craniofacial Sub-specialist Job #JPF11352 Surgery-Plastic / David Geffen School Of Medicine / UCLA Position overview Position title: Plastic & Reconstructive Surgeon (Craniofacial Sub-specialist) Salary range: The posted UC Salary Scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 5, at Scale 7. The salary range for this position is $175,500 - $740,000. This position includes membership in the Health Sciences Compensation Plan , which provides for eligibility for additional compensation. Application Window Open date: September 22, 2026 Next review date: Wednesday, Oct 21, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Jun 30, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The UCLA Division of Plastic Surgery, Department of Surgery, David Geffen School of Medicine at UCLA is seeking a full-time, academic faculty member at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank to join our growing Division of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery in the Health Sciences Clinical Professor series. The ideal candidate will be a fellowship-trained craniofacial surgeon with a strong commitment to clinical excellence, resident education, and clinical or translational research. This role focuses on a comprehensive spectrum of complex facial surgery, spanning pediatric and adult craniofacial care, acute and secondary cranio-maxillofacial trauma, and a dedicated, multidisciplinary program for transgender facial feminization surgery (FFS) and gender-affirming facial procedures. Key Responsibilities Clinical Care: Deliver outstanding, patient- and family-centered surgical care across the full scope of sub-specialized craniofacial surgery. This includes, but is not limited to,  managing complex adult and pediatric congenital anomalies (cleft lip/palate, craniosynostosis), pediatric plastic surgery (vascular anomalies, myelomeningoceles, skin lesions), acquired craniofacial defects (cranioplasty and soft tissue reconstruction), craniomaxillofacial trauma, and facial feminization surgery. Program Development: Serve as a core surgeon and collaborative leader in building the Adult and Pediatric Craniofacial surgical services as well as pediatric plastic surgery across all UCLA Health locations including Westwood, Santa Monica, and West Valley, with the option of being involved and expanding the Facial Gender Affirmation Surgery program. Teaching & Education: Actively participate in the education, training, and mentorship of integrated plastic surgery residents, craniofacial fellows, and medical students including weekly attendance at divisional conferences and resident didactic curricula. Research & Scholarship: Engage in scholarly activity, outcomes-based clinical research, or translational science to advance the field of craniofacial and gender-affirming surgery. The successful candidate will have demonstrated clinical expertise and dedicated training in adult and pediatric craniofacial surgery, maxillofacial trauma management, and facial gender-affirming surgery, as well as a strong record of collegiality, multidisciplinary collaboration, and academic productivity. This position offers clinical, teaching, and scholarly opportunities within a leading academic medical center. The appointee will be expected to participate in the clinical care of patients requiring craniofacial and reconstructive surgical services, contribute to the education and supervision of trainees and students, and engage in scholarly and academic activities. Candidates must possess an MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree and be eligible for an unrestricted California medical license. Applicants must have successfully completed an ACGME-accredited Plastic Surgery Residency program and an accredited or unaccredited Craniofacial Surgery Fellowship. Candidates must be board certified or board eligible by the American Board of Plastic Surgery (ABPS). The salary will be commensurate with academic rank and experience. The University of California seeks to recruit and retain a diverse workforce as a reflection of our commitment to serve the people of California, to maintain the excellence of the University, and to offer our students richly varied disciplines, perspectives and ways of knowing and learning. The shared values of the DGSOM are expressed in the Cultural North Star, which was developed by members of our community and affirms our unswerving commitment to doing what’s right, making things better, and being kind. These are the standards to which we hold ourselves, and one another. Please read more about this important DGSOM program at Cultural North Star . The posted UC Salary Scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 5, at Scale 7. The salary range for this position is $175,500 - $740,000. This position includes membership in the Health Sciences Compensation Plan , which provides for eligibility for additional compensation. Interested applicants must submit their application materials online at UCLA Academic RECRUIT . UC Regents Statement on Ethical Values and Standards of Conduct : All aspects of searches are confidential and all candidates are expected to review and abide by UC Regents Policy 1111 on Statement on Ethical Values and Standards of Conduct. Qualifications Basic qualifications Candidates must possess an MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree and be eligible for an unrestricted California medical license. Additional qualifications Must have successfully completed an ACGME-accredited Plastic Surgery Residency program and an accredited or unaccredited Craniofacial Surgery Fellowship. Candidates must be board certified or board eligible by the American Board of Plastic Surgery (ABPS). Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter (Optional) Statement of Research (Optional) Statement of Teaching (Optional) Reference check authorization release form -   Complete and upload the reference check authorization release form UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service Reference requirements 3-5 required (contact information only) Contact information for references is being requested but will not be used at this time. Final candidates will be notified in advance if the need to contact references is used as part of the final selection process. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11352 Help contact: elizabethmorales@mednet.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Plastic & Reconstructive Surgeon with a Craniofacial Sub-specialist or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
