    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11363": {
      "title": "Director of Teaching and Learning ServicesApply nowto Director of Teaching and Learning Services",
      "description": "Director of Teaching and Learning Services (JPF11363) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Director of Teaching and Learning Services (JPF11363) Director of Teaching and Learning Services Apply now to Director of Teaching and Learning Services Job #JPF11363 General Library / University Library / UCLA Position overview Position title: Director of Teaching and Learning Services Salary range: $78,997 - $162,183 (Associate Librarian – Librarian Rank) Application Window Open date: September 29, 2026 Next review date: Tuesday, Oct 27, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Tuesday, Nov 24, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description Position Description The UCLA Library seeks a highly collaborative leader to serve as unit head for the recently-created Teaching and Learning Services (TLS) department. The director will oversee the department’s programs and services, which include information literacy instruction, core research assistance services, instructional design and assessment, and student engagement. TLS provides direction and leadership for these areas and collaborates with other Library departments for implementation and alignment, including topics such as information literacy related to artificial intelligence, mis/disinformation, and sustainable, scalable, and inclusive pedagogy. This is an opportunity to shape the future of UCLA Library’s teaching and learning programs and lead a talented, creative team whose collective expertise and commitment to service foster strong partnerships across campus. Position Duties Reporting to the AUL for Teaching, Learning, and Research. The incumbent is responsible for the following: Specific duties and responsibilities include: • Develop and lead service strategy for Teaching and Learning Services, including sustainable and scalable information literacy and research help services with focus on: coordination of research help services, foundational and advanced information literacy; student engagement programs, instructional design, and assessment. • Collaborate with Library colleagues to develop and align teaching and learning services and support with departments outside of the TLR division, including Special Collections, International and Area Studies, and the Film and Television Archive. • Develop and maintain strategic relationships with campus partners and entities in support of Library and departmental goals and priorities. • Oversee unit allocated budgets in accordance with UCLA Library Fund Manager expectations. • Oversee recruitment, coaching, mentorship, performance management, and retention of ~18 librarians and staff, both as a direct and indirect supervisor. Serve as Review Initiator (RI) for librarians and support other supervisors and team leads in their responsibilities. Ensure librarian and staff responsibilities and goals are in alignment with unit, division, and library-wide goals and priorities. • Collaborate with colleagues across the Library to inform space-related upgrades and modifications to support services and programming priorities. • Collaborate with colleagues across the Library to ensure collections support evolving teaching and research needs. • Work with AUL to ensure departmental staffing and service models support Library goals and complement Research Services and Access Services foci and service models. • Serve as an active member of the Library’s Management Council. • Work closely with Library Development to identify and steward donors, collections, and endowed gift funds. The successful candidate will be committed to promoting and enhancing diversity through engagement with and promotion of the UCLA True Bruin values and the UCLA Principles of Community ; able to collaborate across departments and divisions; foster an inclusive and team-oriented work environment; adaptable to change; and able to lead teams through change. General Information Professional librarians at UCLA are academic appointees.  This is a non-represented position.  Within the UC System, most librarians are represented by an exclusive bargaining agent, University Council – American Federation of Teachers (UC-AFT).  As academic appointees, librarians are entitled to appropriate professional leave, two days per month of vacation leave, one day per month of sick leave, and all other benefits granted to non-faculty academic personnel.  UC has an excellent retirement system and sponsors a variety of group health, dental, vision, and life insurance plans in addition to other benefits.  Relocation assistance may be provided. Appointees to the librarian series at UC shall have professional backgrounds that demonstrate a high degree of creativity, teamwork, and flexibility.  Such background will normally include a professional degree from an ALA-accredited library and information science graduate program.  In addition to professional competence and quality of service within the library in the primary job, advancement in the librarian series requires professional involvement and contributions outside of the library, and/or university and community service, and/or scholarly activities.  Candidates must show evidence or promise of such contributions. Description of Unit Part of the Teaching, Learning, and Research division, Teaching and Learning Services supports UCLA students, faculty, instructors, staff, and external researchers in attaining the research and critical information literacy skills needed for educational success and lifelong learning across multiple modalities, and connecting users with library resources, collections, and services. The department provides leadership and assessment for the Library’s in-person and online research services, instructional design, and information literacy across the curriculum (including but not limited to specialized subject and format methodologies, primary source, media, data, and AI literacies). TLS collaborates closely with colleagues from across the Library and campus partners to engage the UCLA community with the library’s spaces, services, and collections from orientation through graduation. Description of Institution and Library As one of the world's great public research universities, UCLA integrates education, research, and public service so that each enriches and extends the others. From its beautiful neighborhood campus in a uniquely diverse and vibrant city on the Pacific Rim, teaching and research extend beyond the classroom, office, and lab through active engagement with communities, organizations, projects, and partnerships throughout the region and around the world. UCLA’s diverse community of scholars encompasses nearly 30,000 undergraduates pursuing 125 majors, 13,000 graduate students in fifty-nine research programs, and 4,000 faculty members including Nobel Laureates; Rhodes Scholars; MacArthur Fellows; winners of the Fields Medal, National Medal of Science, Pritzker Prize, and Pulitzer Prize; and recipients of Oscars, Emmys, Tonys, and Golden Globes. UCLA ranks tenth in the Times of London Higher Education World Reputation Rankings, twelfth in the Academic Ranking of World Universities by Shanghai Jiao Tong University, and fifth in the U.S. by Washington Monthly. The National Research Council ranks forty of its graduate and doctoral research programs among its top ten. To enable these accomplished students, faculty, and staff to create, disseminate, and apply knowledge for the benefit of global society, the UCLA Library is re-envisioning how it is acquired, synthesized, and shared across academic audiences and with the public.  It was among the first academic libraries to develop subject-specialist librarians and to launch a program to enhance students’ research skills. Its Special Collections pioneered the acquisition by public institutions of rare and unique books, children’s literature, pulp and detective fiction, works by or about women and minorities, screenplays, architectural plans, and Los Angeles-related materials and today leads the way in collecting archival resources in digital format such as emails and manuscripts.  It has launched innovative data management services and an affordable course materials initiative that have served as models for other libraries. The Library serves UCLA students, faculty, and staff whenever and wherever they need its resources and expertise. Reconfigured, high-tech spaces and services in its ten campus libraries enable users and librarians to explore and work with print and digital materials collaboratively or individually, pursue new lines of inquiry, and develop new pedagogical approaches as well as novel forms of scholarship.  More than 3.5 million people visit annually, while an additional 3.4 million visitors enter online through its virtual front doors. Whether on campus or online, the Library forms the intellectual heart of UCLA, a hub for cutting-edge discovery, scholarship, and instruction. Qualifications Basic qualifications Basic Qualifications • ALA-accredited Master’s Degree in Library or Information Science required at the time of hire; OR Master’s degree (or other advanced degree) in Information Sciences or related field at the time of hire and work experience in a library setting. • Supervisory experience • Experience leading or providing public services in an academic library setting. • Experience with program assessment. • Training or experience with teaching and pedagogy • Experience managing library budgets, either collections, programs, or services. • Understanding of issues and trends impacting academic libraries, including artificial intelligence and online learning. Preferred qualifications Applicants with all the above basic qualifications and any of the following preferred qualifications or professional experiences are strongly encouraged to apply: • Experience in donor relations and stewardship; • Training or experience developing learning objects or instructional materials • Understanding of systems and digital tools for instruction, including course management systems. • 3-5 years of supervisory experience in a library setting. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter -   A cover letter reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlighting your past contributions and future commitments to advancing the UCLA Library strategic directions and organizational values and the UCLA mission and strategic plan , among which are ensuring opportunity for all students and fostering an inclusive community where everyone experiences a sense of belonging. These accomplishments and ambitions should be discussed in the context of describing your experience and qualifications for the position for which you are applying. Reference Contact Information -   3 required (contact information only); Provide names and contact information for three professional references, including current or previous supervisor; contact information only. Reference check authorization release form -   Complete and upload the reference check authorization release form Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11363 Help contact: liliavaldez@library.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Director of Teaching and Learning Services or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11365": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Research Center (HASS) - Assistant Director (JPF11365) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT",
      "description": "Undergraduate Research Center (HASS) - Assistant Director (JPF11365) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Undergraduate Research Center (HASS) - Assistant Director (JPF11365) Undergraduate Research Center (HASS) - Assistant Director Apply now to Undergraduate Research Center (HASS) - Assistant Director Job #JPF11365 UNDERGRADUATE RESEARCH CENTERS / L&S Undergraduate Education / UCLA Position overview Position title: URC-HASS Assistant Director Salary range: $87,616 Review timeline: Posting is live for 30 days.  Applications will be reviewed as soon as they are submitted. Application Window Open date: September 30, 2026 Next review date: Thursday, Oct 15, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Friday, Oct 30, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description Undergraduate Research Center – Humanities, Arts & Social Sciences Academic Administrator III Assistant Director Summary Statement Under the supervision of the Director of the Undergraduate Research Center—Humanities, Arts, and Social Sciences (URC-HASS), the Assistant Director is the primary manager and advisor for the following URC-HASS programs and activities: the Undergraduate Research Fellows Program (URFP), the Undergraduate Research Scholars Program (URSP), the Dean’s Research Fellowship (DRF) and the Summer Research Incubator (SRI). The Assistant Director teaches courses (RP 103: Student Research Forum) and develops and teaches research workshops. The Assistant Director is responsible for the management of all undergraduate and graduate students workers (up to 12, depending on time of year), as well as coordinating university-wide sponsored events and programs. The focus of this position is to support research experiences for undergraduates, including outreach, counseling, and management of introductory and advanced research opportunities. University-level teaching experience is required, as is experience designing, implementing, and evaluating educational, research, or student development programs. A doctoral degree is required; a Ph.D. in the humanities or social sciences is highly preferred. This is a hybrid position. These responsibilities can be divided into the following sections: Program and General Administration Undergraduate Research Fellows Program (URFP): Serve as primary administrator and student advisor for URFP, responsible for program planning and management, student recruitment, and student advising. Oversee admissions process in collaboration with URC-HASS Director and URFP faculty committee. Teach RP 103: Student Research Forum. Collect and analyze data for reports, tracking, and to identify areas for improvement. Work closely with Student Services Advisor to track student progress and implement program components and contribute to evaluation of Student Services Advisor performance. Undergraduate Research Scholars Program (URSP): Serve as primary administrator and student advisor for URSP, responsible for program planning and management, student recruitment, and student advising. Oversee admissions process in collaboration with URC-HASS Director and URSP faculty committee. Coordinate URSP programming and events. Supervise Graduate Research Mentor assigned to the program. Collect and analyze data for reports, tracking, and to identify areas for improvement. Work closely with Student Services Advisor to track student progress and implement program components and contribute to evaluation of Student Services Advisor performance. Dean’s Research Fellowship (DRF): Serve as primary administrator and student advisor for DRF, responsible for program planning and management, student recruitment, and student advising. Oversee admissions process in collaboration with URC-HASS Director and DRF faculty committee. Coordinate DRF programming and events. Supervise Graduate Research Mentor assigned to the program. Design workshops and curricula for small group meetings. Collect and analyze data for reports, tracking, and to identify areas for improvement. Work closely with URC-HASS Student Services Advisor to track student progress and implement program components and contribute to evaluation of Student Services Advisor performance. Summer Research Incubator: Serve as primary administrator for the Summer Research Incubator as part of the UC-HBCU Initiative. Collaborate with URC-HASS Director, liaisons from Morgan State University, and Faculty Advisory Board on program planning and management and student recruitment at both universities. Develop curriculum. Hire, train, and supervise (7+) graduate student mentors. Administer summer program components including workshops, faculty talks, and symposium. Work closely with Administrative and Program Coordinator to track student progress and implement program components. Create yearly report for UC-HBCU Initiative, which includes analyzing data, student tracking, and identifying areas for improvement. Track students yearly after program completion and work with Morgan State University representatives to create ongoing opportunities and pathways for Morgan State students at UCLA. Undergraduate Research Portal in 12twenty: Serve as the primary administrator in URC-HASS for this campus-wide program to provide information about open undergraduate research opportunities. Work closely with staff of the Career Center, UIT, URC-Sciences, the University’s academic departments, and relevant campus centers to manage the services provided by the portal and to promote its use. Organize and oversee advertising and user support for the portal/12twenty. Collect and analyze site and user experience data to identify areas for program improvement. Prepare program documents and progress reports as needed. Undergraduate Research Week: In collaboration with the Director, create programming and initiatives to prepare students for Undergraduate Research Week. Develop and administer program components, including special student panels and Community Engagement and Social Justice Day, and engage with campus partners to meet programmatic goals. Solicit assistance from the URC-HASS and Sciences staff in coordinating and executing the events. Bruin Day and Transfer Bruin Day: Serve as manager for Bruin Day and Transfer Bruin Day events. Work closely with Administrative and Program Coordinator to develop and administer event components. Additional events as assigned by Director. Staff Supervision Directly supervise 5-12 undergraduate and graduate student workers, depending on time of year. Work closely with Administrative and Program Coordinator to hire, train and manage undergraduate and graduate student workers. Serve as the representative for Title VI, student access and Title IX. Teaching Research Practice 103: Student Research Forum (2 units, taught winter quarter): Serve as instructor of record. Additional Research Practice courses as assigned by Director. Mentoring Workshops and Info Sessions: Oversee URC-HASS’s quarterly campus-wide Cornerstone Workshop series. Design, develop, and conduct URC-HASS research and program workshops for and campus partners by request. Collaborate with campus partners to develop new in-person and virtual teaching and learning initiatives. General advisement: Counsel students mentioned in programs above, as well as those in other URC-HASS administered programs, on research opportunities and experiences at UCLA. Advise students on career and post-graduation plans. Coordinate advising with departmental counselors and College counseling units. Letters of Recommendation: Upon request, compose letters of recommendation in support of student applications to graduate and professional schools, internships, scholarships and/or employment. Committee Work Scholarship Committees: Serve on undergraduate research scholarship committees for other UCLA units as requested. Qualifications Basic qualifications • PHD in the Humanities or Social Sciences (or top degree in the appointee's fields or the highest degree that is commonly expected for appointment in the activity) Preferred qualifications Preferred Experience and Skills • At least 1-3 years of administrative work in a higher education, academic, or office environment • Experience designing, implementing, and evaluating educational, research, or student development programs • Excellent interpersonal and relationship-building skills with the ability to work collaboratively with staff, faculty, students, and administrators at UCLA, as well as with external stakeholders • Exceptional organizational and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple programs, events, and deadlines simultaneously across UCLA and other universities • Excellent written communication skills, including report writing, program documentation, and preparation of professional correspondence • Demonstrated ability to develop curriculum, teach at the university level, and create engaging learning experiences in both in-person and virtual environments • Strong oral communication and presentation skills for academic and general audiences • Proven ability to advise, mentor, and support students from diverse academic, cultural, and socioeconomic backgrounds Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter Teaching Statement Reference check authorization release form -   Complete and upload the reference check authorization release form UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service List of References -   Please provide the names, job titles, and email addresses of the writers of the letters of recommendation. Reference requirements 2 letters of reference required Please provide two recommendation letters. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11365 Help contact: zna@college.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Undergraduate Research Center (HASS) - Assistant Director or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
