    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11372": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar in AI and Digital Health Innovation (JPF11372)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Scholar in AI and Digital Health Innovation (JPF11372) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Postdoctoral Scholar in AI and Digital Health Innovation (JPF11372) Postdoctoral Scholar in AI and Digital Health Innovation Apply now to Postdoctoral Scholar in AI and Digital Health Innovation Job #JPF11372 Surgery-General / David Geffen School Of Medicine / UCLA Position overview Position title: Postdoctoral Scholar Salary range: The posted UC Salary Scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 23: Postdoctoral Scholar-Employee, Postdoctoral Scholar-Fellow, Postdoctoral Scholar-Paid Direct, Fiscal Year. Application Window Open date: October 13, 2026 Next review date: Monday, Nov 2, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Thursday, Dec 31, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description Do you want your AI research to move beyond benchmarks and make an impact in real-world healthcare? UCLA’s Center for Advanced Surgical and Interventional Technology (CASIT) , part of the Department of Surgery at the David Geffen School of Medicine, is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Scholar with strong expertise in computer vision, multimodal AI, machine learning, and/or agentic AI to help build the next generation of intelligent digital health solutions. You will work side by side with clinicians, engineers, researchers, and industry partners to transform multimodal health data—including wearable and sensor data, medical images and video, speech, human movement, physiological signals, and clinical information—into AI-powered tools for digital biomarkers, remote monitoring, precision health, clinical decision support, and intelligent surgical systems, with the ultimate goal of translating AI innovation into measurable improvements in patient care. About the Role: We are looking for a researcher with a Ph.D. in Computer Science, Biomedical Engineering, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Data Science, Robotics, Computational Health, or a closely related field and a strong record in AI, machine learning, digital health, or biomedical data science. You will lead interdisciplinary projects that apply advanced computational methods to clinically meaningful problems across the continuum of care—from early detection and functional assessment to intervention, surgery, recovery, and monitoring at home. Prior clinical experience is not required; scientific curiosity, strong quantitative skills, and a genuine interest in learning from clinicians and solving real-world healthcare problems are essential. What You Will Work On: Depending on your strengths and interests, projects may include: Digital biomarkers and multimodal health assessment: Develop AI models that integrate wearable sensors, movement, speech, physiological signals, imaging, video, and clinical data to characterize health status, frailty, resilience, cognitive-motor function, and disease progression. Wearables and remote monitoring: Transform longitudinal data from wearable and connected technologies into clinically actionable measures for monitoring patients in the hospital, at home, and in the community. Computer vision for healthcare: Apply computer vision to medical images, wound images, surgical video, and other visual health data for automated assessment, monitoring, prediction, and decision support. Agentic and generative AI for clinical care: Develop systems based on large language and vision-language models that reason across multimodal data, interact with tools, support clinical workflows, automate documentation, and enable personalized monitoring and care coordination. AI for brain and functional health: Combine speech, mobility, dual-task performance, wearable sensing, and clinical biomarkers to better characterize brain health, intrinsic capacity, and resilience to age- and disease-related decline. Intelligent surgical systems: Develop AI approaches for surgical video understanding, workflow analysis, skill assessment, robotic surgery, human–robot collaboration, and perioperative decision support. Digital interventions and precision health: Use data from telehealth, exercise, rehabilitation, and connected health programs to identify treatment response, engagement, and opportunities for personalized intervention. Key Responsibilities: Lead the design, development, and validation of AI and digital health research projects, from defining clinically meaningful questions to developing and evaluating working prototypes. Develop machine learning, deep learning, multimodal, computer vision, time-series, and/or foundation-model approaches for biomedical and clinical data. Build and curate multimodal datasets and analysis pipelines in compliance with IRB, HIPAA, and institutional requirements. Develop and validate digital biomarkers and AI-enabled decision-support tools with attention to reliability, generalizability, safety, and human oversight. Publish in leading AI, biomedical engineering, digital health, and clinical venues. Contribute to multidisciplinary grant proposals to federal agencies such as NIH, NSF, and ARPA-H, as well as industry-sponsored research. Mentor graduate and undergraduate trainees and collaborate closely with clinicians, engineers, researchers, and industry partners. Qualifications: Required Ph.D. in Computer Science, Biomedical Engineering, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Data Science, Robotics, Computational Health, or a related quantitative field, conferred by the start date. Strong research and publication record in AI, machine learning, computer vision, digital health, biomedical engineering, medical AI, or a closely related area. Expertise in one or more areas such as deep learning, computer vision, multimodal learning, time-series analysis, signal processing, foundation models, digital biomarkers, or agentic AI. Strong programming and data-analysis skills, particularly in Python and modern machine-learning frameworks such as PyTorch or equivalent. Experience working with complex, high-dimensional datasets. Clear scientific communication skills and a collaborative style suited to multidisciplinary research. Preferred Experience with wearable sensors, digital biomarkers, medical imaging, speech analytics, human movement, remote monitoring, or physiological signals. Experience with multimodal or longitudinal data, including fusion of video, sensor, audio, imaging, or clinical information. Knowledge of large language models, vision-language models, generative AI, or agentic AI methods such as tool use, planning, retrieval-augmented generation, or multi-agent systems. Experience with surgical or medical data, including surgical video, endoscopy, medical imaging, robotics, or perioperative datasets. Experience deploying models in real-world or real-time settings, including efficient inference or edge computing. Interest in trustworthy AI for healthcare, including uncertainty estimation, explainability, bias, privacy, safety, and human-AI interaction. Experience contributing to grant proposals, mentoring students, or translating research toward clinical validation. The posted UC Salary Scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 23: Postdoctoral Scholar-Employee, Postdoctoral Scholar-Fellow, Postdoctoral Scholar-Paid Direct, Fiscal Year. About Us: CASIT brings clinicians, engineers, data scientists, and researchers together to develop technologies that improve health across the continuum of care. Our work extends from AI-enabled surgical and interventional systems to wearable sensing, digital biomarkers, remote patient monitoring, speech and mobility analytics, wound monitoring, telehealth, healthy aging, and functional resilience. We are particularly interested in combining multimodal data to understand not only disease, but also function, recovery, resilience, and changes in health over time. Across all of our programs, the goal is the same: to move beyond proof-of-concept algorithms and develop technologies that can be clinically validated, deployed in real-world settings, and ultimately improve patient outcomes. Why Join Us? Direct Clinical Environment: Work within UCLA Health and the Department of Surgery, side by side with clinicians, and ground your research in meaningful real-world healthcare problems. Broad Digital Health Portfolio: Contribute to projects spanning AI, digital biomarkers, wearables, computer vision, remote monitoring, precision health, and intelligent surgical systems. Unique Multimodal Data: Work with combinations of speech, movement, wearable sensors, images, video, physiological signals, and clinical information. Translational Impact: Take projects beyond algorithm development toward prototypes, clinical validation, implementation, and real-world deployment. Mentorship and Leadership: Work under the direction of Dr. Bijan Najafi, Professor of Surgery and Research Director of CASIT, with opportunities to lead projects, mentor trainees, and develop an independent research program. Career Development: Build a strong profile for academic or industry careers through hands-on experience in scientific leadership, grant writing, multidisciplinary collaboration, and clinical translation. Joining our team offers a rare combination of cutting-edge AI research, digital health innovation, direct clinical exposure, and real-world translational impact. The shared values of the DGSOM are expressed in the Cultural North Star, which was developed by members of our community and affirms our unswerving commitment to doing what’s right, making things better, and being kind. These are standards to which we hold ourselves, and one another. Please read more about this important DGSOM program . Interested candidates can apply through the UC Recruit System . For additional information please contact: Bijan Najafi, Ph.D. Professor of Surgery & BioEngineering Research Director, Center for Advanced Surgical & Interventional Technology (CASIT) bnajafi@mednet.ucla.edu . Qualifications Basic qualifications Ph.D. (or equivalent) in Computer Science, Biomedical Engineering, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Data Science, Robotics, Computational Health, or a related quantitative field Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. with a full publication list Cover Letter -   1 to 2 page cover letter describing your research experience and how your expertise could advance AI and digital health innovation. Reprint of Publications  -   2 to 3 reprints of repesentative publications Optional -   Links to code repositories, software, project websites, or demos are encouraged (Optional) UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service Reference requirements 3 required (contact information only) Contact information for references is being requested but will not be used at this time. Final candidates will be notified in advance if the need to contact references is used as part of the final selection process. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11372 Help contact: scmunoz@mednet.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Postdoctoral Scholar in AI and Digital Health Innovation or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11375": {
      "title": "Assistant Project ScientistApply nowto Assistant Project Scientist",
      "description": "Assistant Project Scientist (JPF11375) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Assistant Project Scientist (JPF11375) Assistant Project Scientist Apply now to Assistant Project Scientist Job #JPF11375 EARTH, PLANETARY, AND SPACE SCIENCES / L&S Physical Sciences / UCLA Position overview Salary range: The [posted UC salary scales]( https://ucop.edu/academic-personnel-programs/compensation/index.html ) set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 37-B The salary range for this position is $79,400 - $92,200. \"Off-scale salaries\", i.e. a salary that is higher than the published system-wide salary at the designated rank and step, are offered when necessary to meet competitive conditions. Application Window Open date: October 7, 2026 Next review date: Friday, Nov 6, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Friday, Nov 6, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date. Position description The Department of Earth, Planetary, and Space Sciences at the University of California, Los Angeles, seeks applicants for an Assistant Project Scientist in the Experimental Space Physics research group. The candidate must hold a PhD in Physics, Geophysics, or Engineering at the time of hiring.  The candidate must have detailed knowledge of space plasma physics and be well-versed in energetic particle instrumentation design, fabrication, testing, ground and in-flight calibration, and data analysis related to these instruments. In particular, the candidate will have demonstrated experience in the above areas through peer-reviewed publications, delivery of test reports and test or flight articles with successful performance, per requirements. Participation in NASA or NSF flight projects and experience with product assurance methods and processes is desirable. Type of Supervision: Principal Investigator to provide top-level assignment of responsibilities and tasks. Daily workload assigned and monitored by immediate supervisor. Energetic particle instrument design and testing (75%): Under the guidance and close supervision of the PI, engineers, and staff, the candidate will: design and develop the instrument(s) by performing all necessary testing, analysis, and interpretation of the data; develop and operate novel ground support equipment, including but not limited to detector evaluation using radioactive sources and thermal vacuum testing; update and maintain all testing/QA documentation, and produce reports. Independent Research and Research Support (25%): Under the supervision of the PI, the candidate will have the opportunity to conduct independent research projects, prepare proposals for federal funding, and author relevant manuscripts for publication. Assist faculty, staff, and students with miscellaneous instruments, hardware, and software (e.g. IDL, Python, Matlab, etc.) for laboratory experiments, and/or developing additional space plasma instrumentation for spaceflight. The candidate will also support the development of new analytical techniques in consultation with the PI. Candidates must provide a (a) curriculum vitae, (b) cover letter, (c) statement of research interests (max 2 pages), (d) UCLA Mission Statement and (e) three to five references, including names and contact information. The posted UC salary scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 37-B. The salary range for this position is $79,400 - $92,200. \"Off-scale salaries\", i.e. a salary that is higher than the published system-wide salary at the designated rank and step, are offered when necessary to meet competitive conditions Applications will be accepted via the UC Recruit website https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11375 beginning October 7th, 2026. The deadline to submit your application is November 6th, 2026. Inquiries may be directed to Emmanuel Masongsong ( emasongsong@epss.ucla.edu ). Qualifications Basic qualifications The candidate must hold a PhD in Physics, Geophysics, or Engineering at the time of hiring. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter (Optional) Statement of Research (Optional) UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service Reference requirements 3-5 required (contact information only) Reference contact information is being requested of applicants. References may be asked to provide a letter of recommendation. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11375 Help contact: leah.lee@epss.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Assistant Project Scientist or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
