    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11383": {
      "title": "Pediatrics Neurology",
      "description": "Pediatrics Neurology - Research Series - Jeste Lab (JPF11383) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Pediatrics Neurology - Research Series - Jeste Lab (JPF11383) Pediatrics Neurology - Research Series - Jeste Lab Apply now to Pediatrics Neurology - Research Series - Jeste Lab Job #JPF11383 Pediatrics-Neurology / David Geffen School Of Medicine / UCLA Position overview Salary range: The posted UC salary scales set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 13-B . The salary range for this position is $96,700 to $257,100. Application Window Open date: October 19, 2026 Next review date: Thursday, Nov 19, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Tuesday, Oct 19, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description Position overview The Jeste Lab in the UCLA Department of Pediatrics seeks a Researcher to serve as a senior scientific partner to the Principal Investigator. This person will help lead a translational research program on early biomarkers, diagnosis and intervention in autism spectrum disorder (ASD), intellectual disability (ID) and genetic neurodevelopmental syndromes. The role combines independent scientific work with lab leadership. The Researcher will direct the lab's EEG and eye-tracking biomarker research, co-write grants and manuscripts with the PI, oversee daily lab operations, and supervise postdoctoral fellows, graduate students and research staff on their own projects and analyses. About the lab The lab is led by Shafali Jeste, MD, Professor and Mattel Executive Endowed Chair of the Department of Pediatrics, Executive Medical Director of Pediatrics in the UCLA Health System, and Executive Director of the UCLA Children's Discovery and Innovation Institute. Dr. Jeste is a behavioral child neurologist with more than 160 peer-reviewed publications. The lab's work aims to improve precision in the diagnosis and treatment of neurodevelopmental disorders. Current and recent areas include: • Early predictors of autism that integrate EEG and other biomarkers with behavioral measures to identify atypical development before diagnosis • Biomarker and outcome measure development in genetic syndromes, including Dup15q and Angelman syndromes • Early intervention trials, including the first randomized controlled trial of behavioral intervention for infants with a genetic form of autism • Aligning Research to Impact Autism (ARIA), a large initiative Dr. Jeste has co-led since 2024 to accelerate discovery and therapeutic development The research is closely tied to multidisciplinary clinical programs for children with complex neurodevelopmental disorders. Funding comes from the National Institutes of Health, the Department of Defense, the Simons Foundation, the Angelman Syndrome Foundation, the Dup15q Alliance and the Sergey Brin Family Foundation. Responsibilities Scientific leadership • Partner with the PI to set the lab's scientific direction, develop new research aims and design studies • Lead acquisition and analysis of EEG data (resting-state spectral power, event-related potentials, connectivity) and related biomarkers such as eye tracking • Establish and maintain standardized pipelines for data processing, quality control and harmonization across studies and sites • Pursue an independent line of inquiry within the lab's program, with first- and senior-author opportunities Grants and publications • Co-write federal and foundation grant applications, including NIH R01 and U-series mechanisms, renewals and progress reports • Lead and co-author manuscripts, conference presentations and data-sharing submissions • Contribute to reviews, white papers and consortium deliverables, including ARIA Lab management • Oversee daily lab operations, study timelines, staffing and budgets in coordination with the PI • Manage IRB protocols, regulatory compliance and data security • Coordinate with clinical programs, collaborating sites and family foundations Mentorship and supervision • Supervise postdoctoral fellows, graduate students, undergraduates and research staff on their independent projects and analyses • Train lab members in EEG and eye-tracking acquisition with infants and children with ASD and ID, and in analytic methods • Review analysis plans, code and drafts, and help trainees develop their own funding applications Interested candidates can apply online at: UCLA Academic RECRUIT-JPF11383 UC Regents Statement on Ethical Values and Standards of Conduct: All aspects of searches are confidential and all candidates are expected to review and abide by UC Regents Policy 1111 on Statement on Ethical Values and Standards of Conduct ) Cultural North Star: The shared values of the DGSOM are expressed in the Cultural North Star, which was developed by members of our community and affirms our unswerving commitment to doing what’s right, making things better, and being kind. These are the standards to which we hold ourselves, and one another. Please read more about this important DGSOM program at Medical School | Cultural North Star | UCLA Medical School . Qualifications Basic qualifications • PhD in neuroscience, psychology, biomedical engineering, cognitive science or a related field.  Candidates with a master's degree and substantial equivalent research experience will be considered. Additional qualifications • Hands-on experience acquiring and analyzing functional imaging data, with EEG strongly preferred • Research experience with neurodevelopmental populations, particularly children with ASD and/or ID • Proficiency in programming, such as MATLAB, Python or R, and some experience in EEG analysis tools such as EEGLAB, MNE or FieldTrip • A record of first-author peer-reviewed publications • Experience supervising or mentoring students, trainees or staff • Strong scientific writing and the judgment to work independently while advancing a shared research program Preferred qualifications • Experience with eye tracking (e.g., Tobii, EyeLink) or other candidate biomarkers • Work with infants, toddlers or minimally verbal children • Research in genetic neurodevelopmental syndromes • Contributions to funded grant applications, or a record of independent fellowship or foundation funding • Experience with multisite studies, data harmonization or biomarkers in clinical trials • Advanced statistical methods, including longitudinal and mixed-effects modeling or machine learning Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter -   Please describe research experience, analytic expertise and interest in the position. Representative Publications -   Representative Publications (up to three) Reference requirements 3 required (contact information only) Applicants must provide the names and contact information of their references. The hiring unit will request confidential letters from the references of those applicants who are under serious consideration. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11383 Help contact: rgallegos@mednet.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Pediatrics Neurology - Research Series - Jeste Lab or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
