    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11418": {
      "title": "Project Scientists (Open Rank) in Computer Science for AY 26-27 (JPF11418)",
      "description": "Project Scientists (Open Rank) in Computer Science for AY 26-27 (JPF11418) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Project Scientists (Open Rank) in Computer Science for AY 26-27 (JPF11418) Project Scientists (Open Rank) in Computer Science for AY 26-27 Apply now to Project Scientists (Open Rank) in Computer Science for AY 26-27 Job #JPF11418 Computer Science / Henry Samueli School Engr & Ap / UCLA Position overview Position title: Assistant Project Scientist, Associate Project Scientist, Project Scientist Salary range: Please refer to Table 38B for salary range for this position. Application Window Open date: October 26, 2026 Next review date: Monday, Nov 2, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Jun 30, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description UCLA Samueli School of Engineering Department of Computer Science Open Rank Project Scientist – Assistant, Associate, and Full Position Description The Department of Computer Science (CS) at the UCLA Samueli School of Engineering invites applications for an open-rank Project Scientist position at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Project Scientist level. Rank will be determined based on the candidate's qualifications, experience, and professional accomplishments. The department seeks highly motivated individuals to contribute to ongoing and emerging research initiatives across a broad range of areas within Computer Science. Areas of research may include, but are not limited to, artificial intelligence and machine learning, computer engineering, communications and signal processing, circuits and embedded systems, semiconductor devices, photonics, electromagnetics, robotics, control systems, and other related fields. Successful candidates will work independently and collaboratively with faculty, researchers, students, and interdisciplinary research teams to advance the department's research objectives. Responsibilities Responsibilities may vary depending on the research program, assigned projects, and appointment rank, and may include: Conduct independent and collaborative research in specialized areas of computer science. Design, develop, and implement research methodologies, experiments, computational models, and analytical approaches. Analyze and interpret research findings and contribute to the preparation of scholarly publications, technical reports, and presentations. Contribute to the development of research proposals, grant applications, and other activities supporting externally funded research programs. Collaborate with faculty, research staff, graduate students, and external partners to achieve project objectives. Provide technical expertise and research guidance to students and other members of the research team, as appropriate. Present research findings at academic conferences, professional meetings, and other scientific forums. Support the coordination and execution of research activities, including project planning, documentation, and reporting. Maintain high standards of research integrity, safety, and compliance with applicable University policies and sponsor requirements. Qualifications Basic Qualifications (Required at Time of Application) Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering,  or a closely related field. Additional Qualifications Demonstrated research experience in a field relevant to Computer Science, Computer Engineering, or Electrical Engineering. Ability to conduct research independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary environment. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and technical communication skills. Demonstrated ability to contribute to scholarly publications, research presentations, or other scientific outputs. Qualifications and research accomplishments appropriate to the proposed appointment rank. Rank Considerations Assistant Project Scientist Candidates at the Assistant Project Scientist rank are expected to demonstrate the ability to make meaningful contributions to research projects, exercise independent scientific judgment, and develop a record of research accomplishments appropriate to their career stage. Associate Project Scientist Candidates at the Associate Project Scientist rank are expected to have an established record of research accomplishments, demonstrated expertise in their field, and the ability to independently undertake significant research responsibilities and contribute to the advancement of research programs. Project Scientist (Full Rank) Candidates at the Full Project Scientist rank are expected to demonstrate a substantial record of research accomplishments, advanced expertise in their area of specialization, and a high level of independence in conducting and advancing complex research activities. Candidates should have a demonstrated ability to make significant contributions to established or emerging research programs. Appointment Information Appointments will be made at the Assistant Project Scientist, Associate Project Scientist, or Full Project Scientist rank, commensurate with the selected candidate's qualifications and experience. Positions may be full-time or part-time, depending on programmatic needs and funding availability. Appointments are subject to applicable University of California policies and procedures governing the Project Scientist series. Department : https://www.cs.ucla.edu/ Qualifications Basic qualifications Basic Qualifications (Required at Time of Application) Applicants must hold a Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in Computer Science, or a closely related field, or have an equivalent combination of education and research experience appropriate to the Project Scientist series. Applicants must demonstrate research experience in an area relevant to Computer Science, Computer Engineering, or Electrical Engineering, with qualifications and scholarly accomplishments appropriate to the Assistant Project Scientist, Associate Project Scientist, or Full Project Scientist rank. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter (Optional) Statement of Research Reference check authorization release form -   Complete and upload the reference check authorization release form UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service Statement of Teaching (Optional) Reference requirements 3-5 required (contact information only) Applicants must provide the names and contact information of three professional references who can speak to their research experience, scholarly accomplishments, technical expertise, and qualifications for the position. References may be contacted during the selection process. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11418 Help contact: madelen@cs.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Job location Los Angeles, CA Apply now to Project Scientists (Open Rank) in Computer Science for AY 26-27 or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, Los Angeles is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, Los Angeles is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic RECRUIT Personnel System University of California, Los Angeles Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11419": {
      "title": "Project Scientist",
      "description": "Project Scientist - Microscopy Data Science and High-Performance Computing (JPF11419) - UCLA Academic RECRUIT Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Project Scientist - Microscopy Data Science and High-Performance Computing (JPF11419) Project Scientist - Microscopy Data Science and High-Performance Computing Apply now to Project Scientist - Microscopy Data Science and High-Performance Computing Job #JPF11419 Calif Nanosystems Institute / California Nanosystems Institu / UCLA Position overview Position title: Project Scientist Salary range: The posted UC salary tables set the minimum pay determined by rank and step at appointment. See Table 37B . The salary range for this position is $111,500 -$119,600. “Off-scale salaries”, i.e. a salary that is higher than the published system-wide salary at the designated rank and step, are offered when necessary to meet competitive conditions. Application Window Open date: October 22, 2026 Next review date: Saturday, Nov 21, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Saturday, Nov 21, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date. Position description The California NanoSystems Institute (CNSI) at UCLA is seeking a Project Scientist to support operation of its Technology Centers and administration of the Data Analysis and Science Hub (DASH) high-performance computing cluster with specific focus on experimental workflows for cryo-electron microscopy: single-particle analysis (SPA), three-dimensional reconstruction, and structure determination in the Electron Imaging Center for Nanosystems (EICN). The position combines direct scientific support and user training with computational method development and collaborative research. The Project Scientist will support end-to-end processing and analysis of single-particle cryo-EM datasets. The incumbent will develop and maintain reproducible processing workflows. As a scientific and technical point of contact, the Project Scientist will work with users, collaborators, and staff to translate research questions into feasible analysis strategies, evaluate data quality, recommend follow-up data collection, train users, troubleshoot workflows, interpret results, and coordinate analysis projects. The Project Scientist will serve as the scientific administrator and primary user-support lead for DASH in collaboration with the CNSI UCLA information technology group. The incumbent will administer scientific applications and user-facing services, software environments, access and resource allocations within delegated authority, computational workflows, resource monitoring, storage practices, documentation, and training, while partnering with the IT group on core infrastructure, hardware, operating systems, networking, cybersecurity, backup and recovery, and system maintenance. The successful candidate will perform independent and collaborative research under the supervision of Adam Stieg, Associate Director of CNSI. Effort will be divided among cryo-EM user services, DASH administration, and computational method development in response to Institute priorities and sponsored-project needs. The candidate will contribute to publications, presentations, proposals, workshops, and activities that expand capabilities and build community expertise. The Project Scientist position is a 2-year non-senate position, with the possibility of renewal. Duties and Responsibilities: Cryo-EM Data Analysis and User Support: • Provide scientific consultation and feasibility assessments for internal and external users; translate research goals and available datasets into appropriate single-particle cryo-EM processing, reconstruction, validation, and modeling workflows. • Perform end-to-end single-particle cryo-EM data processing, including motion correction, contrast transfer function estimation, particle picking, two-dimensional classification, initial model generation, three-dimensional classification, refinement, map post-processing, and validation. • Assess micrograph, particle, map, and model quality; identify artifacts and limitations; and recommend reprocessing strategies or additional data collection in coordination with EICN microscopy staff. • Provide map interpretation, atomic model building and refinement, structural validation, quantitative analysis, and visualization services. • Coordinate user requests and analysis projects, including data transfer, project planning, priorities, milestones, deliverables, and follow-up communication. • Train users in cryo-EM data processing, structure determination, DASH use, data management, reproducible analysis practices, and interpretation of results. • Develop, maintain, and disseminate standard operating procedures, workflow templates, training materials, troubleshooting guides, and technical documentation. • Track cryo-EM analysis activity, computational use, and scientific output; maintain records needed for operational reporting, facility review, and documentation of research impact. DASH High-Performance Computing Administration and User Support: • Administer the day-to-day scientific and user-facing operation of the Data Analysis and Science Hub (DASH) in collaboration with the appropriate UCLA information technology group. • Manage user onboarding and offboarding, accounts, access permissions, research groups, resource allocations, storage quotas, and job-submission practices within delegated authority and established UCLA policies. • Install, configure, test, document, and update cryo-EM and related scientific software, software modules, containers, libraries, dependencies, and GPU-enabled applications. • Maintain reproducible computational environments and records of software versions, configuration changes, workflow dependencies, and validated operating procedures. • Monitor workload-scheduler activity, CPU and GPU utilization, memory use, storage capacity, and job performance; diagnose failed or inefficient jobs and assist users with resource selection and workflow optimization. • Coordinate with the IT group on hardware, operating systems, networking, cybersecurity, backup and recovery, maintenance windows, upgrades, outages, and resolution of infrastructure incidents. • Develop and implement practices for secure and efficient data transfer, organization, storage, sharing, retention, and archival, consistent with applicable UCLA and sponsor requirements. • Maintain DASH policies, user guides, training materials, service records, utilization summaries, and capacity assessments needed for user support and resource planning. Research and Computational Method Development Duties and Responsibilities: • Design, implement, and validate project-specific strategies for single-particle cryo-EM structure determination, including approaches for heterogeneous, flexible, preferred-orientation, low-abundance, or limited-resolution datasets. • Develop, document, and maintain scripts, workflow templates, and automated or semi-automated pipelines that improve the speed, scalability, reproducibility, and reliability of cryo-EM analysis. • Evaluate and benchmark emerging cryo-EM software, algorithms, machine-learning methods, and computing approaches and integrate validated tools into EICN workflows where they provide measurable benefit. • Establish quality-control, benchmarking, and validation procedures for data quality, particle populations, reconstruction reliability, resolution, map interpretability, model-to-map agreement, throughput, and reproducibility. • Apply computational methods to collaborative and user-driven research projects to produce structures, analyses, visualizations, and scientific interpretations. • Disseminate results through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, technical meetings, workshops, training materials, and contributions to grant proposals. Additional Duties and Responsibilities: • Serve as a liaison among EICN users, faculty investigators, center staff, CNSI administration, UCLA information technology personnel, external collaborators, companies, and software or equipment vendors. • Participate in demonstrations, seminars, workshops, open houses, and other activities that communicate EICN cryo-EM and computational capabilities and promote effective facility use. • Contribute to operational and scientific reports, facility reviews, proposal preparation, and documentation of user outcomes and research impact. • Work efficiently across multiple user, infrastructure, and research projects while maintaining clear priorities, timelines, records, and communication. • Cultivate an inclusive, collegial, service-oriented, and collaborative environment for users, staff, and partners. • Complete required safety, cybersecurity, data-management, and compliance training and follow applicable UCLA, CNSI, and EICN policies and procedures. • Perform other duties consistent with the Project Scientist role and the evolving needs of EICN. Qualifications Basic qualifications Ph.D in structural biology, biochemistry, biophysics, computational biology, bioinformatics, chemistry, physics, computer science, engineering, or a closely related field by the anticipated start date. Additional qualifications ● Demonstrated experience in single-particle cryo-electron microscopy and end-to-end data processing, three-dimensional reconstruction, and structure determination. ● Experience using major cryo-EM processing software such as cryoSPARC, RELION, cisTEM, EMAN2, or equivalent tools. ● Experience working in Linux-based, GPU-enabled, or high-performance computing environments, including scientific software installation, workload scheduling, user support, and computational troubleshooting. ● Working knowledge of a relevant programming or scripting language, such as Python or shell scripting, and experience developing reproducible or automated data-analysis workflows. ● Experience organizing, transferring, processing, and documenting large scientific datasets and maintaining clear records of analysis parameters, software versions, and data provenance. ● Ability to assess cryo-EM data quality, diagnose processing and software problems, recognize artifacts and limitations, and communicate findings to a multidisciplinary audience. ● Demonstrated record of peer-reviewed publications or other significant contributions in cryo-EM, structural biology, computational science, or a related field. ● Ability to work independently and collaboratively, manage multiple projects, and train scientists at different levels of experience in a shared academic research setting. ● Excellent written, oral, interpersonal, organizational, and technical communication skills. Preferred qualifications ● Experience administering or providing advanced technical support for a shared high-performance computing cluster, including workload managers such as Slurm or an equivalent system, software modules or containers, GPU computing, resource monitoring, and storage management. ● Experience maintaining reproducible scientific software environments and documenting application versions, dependencies, configurations, and system changes. ● Experience with atomic model building, refinement, and validation using tools such as Coot, Phenix, ISOLDE, or equivalent software. ● Experience analyzing Titan Krios and direct-electron-detector data and developing advanced or automated methods for challenging single-particle cryo-EM datasets. ● Experience providing scientific services, user consultation, training, or computational support in a shared research facility and contributing to proposals, workshops, facility operations, or outreach. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter Statement of Research Reference check authorization release form -   Complete and upload the reference check authorization release form UCLA Mission Statement -   As the nation's premier public research university, UC's mission is the creation, dissemination, preservation and application of knowledge for the betterment of our global society. We have a particular responsibility to the people of California which we express in the excellence of the education we provide, the impact of the research we do, the comprehensive, life-saving medical services we provide, and the public service mission we are devoted to. The University of California promotes the social mobility of its students, equips them with the tools and experience that furthers their ambitions, and regards their accomplishments across the life span as evidence of the profoundly positive impact of higher education. The UCLA campus has expressed these goals in its strategic plan as follows: Deepen our engagement with Los Angeles Expand our reach as a global university Enhance our research and creative activities Elevate how we teach Become a more effective institution Prompt for candidates for recruitment: Reflecting on your personal and professional experiences, highlight your past contributions and future commitments to advancing UCLA's mission as embodied in the 2023-28 strategic plan. These accomplishments and ambitions may be discussed in the context of describing your teaching, scholarship, and service Statement of Teaching (Optional) Reference requirements 2-3 required (contact information only) References may be contacted to verify experience. Apply link: https://recruit.apo.ucla.edu/JPF11419 Help contact: tmonay@cnsi.ucla.edu About UCLA As a University employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time.  Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to